Implementing a text editor with multiple list items (multitree) in Angular involves several steps. Here's a basic outline of how you might approach this:

### Step 1: Set Up Your Project

First, make sure you have Angular CLI installed and create a new project if you haven't already.

```bash
ng new my-text-editor-app
cd my-text-editor-app
```

### Step 2: Create the Main Text Editor Component

Create a component for your main text editor. This will be where most of your logic resides.

```bash
ng generate component text-editor
```

Inside `text-editor.component.ts`, import necessary modules and set up your interface:

```typescript
import { Component } from '@angular/core';

@Component({
  selector: 'app-text-editor',
  templateUrl: './text-editor.component.html',
  styleUrls: ['./text-editor.component.css']
})
export class TextEditorComponent {
  // Add properties as needed
}
```

### Step 3: Define the Model for Multitree List Items

For each item in your multitree, define an object that represents its content and structure.

```typescript
// src/app/text-editor/text-editor.component.ts

interface TreeNode {
  name: string;
  children?: TreeNode[];
}

const treeNodes = [
  {
    name: "Root Node",
    children: [
      { name: "Child 1" },
      { name: "Child 2" }
    ]
  },
  {
    name: "Another Root Node",
    children: [
      { name: "Grandchild 1" },
      { name: "Grandchild 2" }
    ]
  }
];

let currentNode = treeNodes[0]; // Start at root node
```

### Step 4: Implement the MultiTree Display Logic

In your template (`text-editor.component.html`), use Angular Material or any other UI library to display the multi-tree structure. Bind the selected node using two-way data binding.

```html
<!-- src/app/text-editor/text-editor.component.html -->

<mat-tree [data]="treeNodes">
  <mat-tree-node *ngFor="let child of currentNode.children" [node]="child"></mat-tree-node>
</mat-tree>

<button mat-button color="primary" (click)="moveToNextNode()">Move to Next Node</button>
```

### Step 5: Handle Navigation Between Nodes

Add a function in your service or directly in the component to handle navigation between nodes based on user actions like clicking buttons.

```typescript
// src/app/text-editor/services/tree.service.ts

@Injectable()
export class TreeService {
  moveToNextNode() {
    const nextNode = treeNodes.find(node => node.name === 'Next Node');
    if (nextNode) {
      currentNode = nextNode;
    }
  }

  // Other methods...
}
```

### Step 6: Update Current Node Based on User Actions

Finally, update the current node whenever a user navigates to a different node.

```typescript
// src/app/text-editor/text-editor.component.ts

onNodeClicked(event: MatTreeNodeEvent) {
  const { index } = event;
  if (index >= 0 && index < treeNodes.length) {
    currentNode = treeNodes[index];
  }
}
```

### Step 7: Test Your Application

Run your application and test the text editor with multiple tree-like structures. Click through the nodes to ensure they navigate correctly.

### Additional Considerations

- **Validation**: Ensure your validation is handled appropriately when navigating back and forth.
- **Error Handling**: Provide robust error handling for cases where the user attempts to navigate outside the valid path.
- **Performance**: Optimize performance, especially for large datasets, by lazy loading and caching unnecessary data.

